{"page_content": "until total extinction.  For constant ambient oxygen concentration, larger flames (with \nhigher fuel flow rates) radiatively extinguish faster than smaller flames (with lower flow \nrates) due to higher radiative losses and decreasing chemical heat release.  A thorough \nanalysis of low flow rate flames was completed in order to investigate the existence of \nsteady-state spherical flames, which has been a subject of debate in literature.  It was \nfound that steady state microgravity spherical diffusion flames can exist.  Furthermore, it \nwas found that ethylene microgravity spherical diffusion flames at 1 bar extinguish at a \ncritical extinction point defined by an extinction flame temperature and an extinction \nreactant mass flux at the flame.  These analyses are described in the manuscripts \naccepted for the 39th International Symposium on Combustion cited below.", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 51}}